"day","H7227":"many","H5750":"still","H3754":"vineyard","H6419":"to pray","H310":"after","H162":"alas!","H136":"Lord","H3069":"YHWH/God","H6213":"do","H8064":"heaven","H3581":"reptile","H1419":"large","H2220":"arm","H5186":"to stretch","H6381":"to wonder","H4480":"from","H2617":"kindness","H505":"thousand","H7999":"to complete","H5771":"crime","H1":"father","H2436":"embrace","H410":"God","H1368":"mighty man","H8034":"name","H6098":"counsel","H5950":"deed","H6491":"to open","H1870":"conduct","H120":"man","H376":"man","H6529":"fruit","H4611":"deed","H7760":"make","H226":"miraculous","H4159":"wonder","H4714":"Egypt","H3318":"come","H5971":"people","H2389":"strong","H248":"arm","H4172":"fear","H7650":"to swear","H2100":"flowing","H2461":"milk","H1706":"honey","H3423":"possess","H8085":"hear","H6963":"voice","H8451":"instruction","H1980":"went","H7122":"meet","H7451":"harmful","H5550":"mound","H6440":"before","H2719":"sword","H7458":"famine","H1698":"pestilence","H589":"I","H1320":"flesh","H3651":"right","H3341":"to kindle","H784":"fire","H8313":"to burn","H6999":"burn","H1406":"roof","H1168":"Baal","H5258":"to pour","H5262":"drink offering","H312":"another","H3707":"to provoke","H389":"surely","H5271":"youth","H4639":"work","H639":"anger","H2534":"rage","H1129":"to build","H5493":"remove","H1992":"they(masc.)","H8269":"ruler","H3548":"priest","H6437":"to turn","H6203":"neck","H3925":"teach","H7925":"to rise","H369":"nothing","H4148":"discipline","H8251":"abomination","H7121":"call to","H2930":"to defile","H1116":"high place","H1516":"Valley","H2011":"(Topheth of) Hinnom","H5674":"to pass","H1323":"daughter","H4432":"Molech","H5927":"rise","H3820":"heart","H8441":"abomination","H2398":"to sin","H6258":"now","H6908":"to gather","H5080":"to banish","H7110":"wrath","H7725":"return","H4725":"place","H983":"security","H259":"one","H3372":"to fear","H2896":"pleasant","H3772":"cut","H1285":"covenant","H5769":"enduring","H3190":"be good","H3374":"fear","H3824":"heart","H1115":"lest","H7797":"to rejoice","H2895":"be pleasing","H5193":"to plant","H571":"faithful","H5315":"soul","H595":"I","H8077":"devastation","H929":"animal","H5439":"around","H2022":"mount","H8219":"Shephelah","H5045":"Negeb","H7622":"captivity"},"ccc":{"10":[1295],"17":[269]},"cccp":{"269":"__P18#","1295":"__P3R#"},"com":[["haydock",1,null,"haydock","Tenth. The city had been besieged about a year, (C. xxxix. 1.) and the prophet continued to admonish the king and people of their fate. C. xxxiv. (Calmet) — He bought land, to shew that they should one day return. (Worthington)"],["haydock",2,null,"haydock","Court, at large, so that people might come to him freely. He was not chained or in prison, as C. xxxvii. 15. and xxxviii. 6. 13. (Calmet)"],["haydock",4,null,"haydock","Eyes. They were afterwards put out at Reblatha, (Haydock) so that he could not see Babylon. Ezec. xii. 13. (Worthington) — Sedecias was imprisoned, and buried there by the Jews. C. xxxiv. 5. and 12."],["haydock",7,null,"haydock","Next. Priests could sell only to their fellow priests. Lev. xxv. 34. (St. Jerome) — Others might dispose of their landed property to any, until the year of jubilee. The nearest relation had the first offer, but he was not obliged to purchase. Jeremias now consented, to shew that the people should return to possess the land, though it was now in the enemy's hands. (Calmet) — Thus a Roman purchased the land on which Hannibal was encamped, despising his power and threats. V. Max. iii. 8. Livy xxvi. (Haydock)"],["haydock",8,null,"haydock","Came into the city clandestinely; or rather he entered before the siege. (Calmet)"],["haydock",9,null,"haydock","Silver. Hebrew \"seventeen sicles of silver.\" (Haydock) — Coin was not yet used. (Calmet)"],["haydock",11,null,"haydock","Outside. \"There was another copy left open for inspection, (v. 14.) which custom still prevails,\" says (St. Jerome; Haydock)"],["haydock",14,null,"haydock","Days, safe from dampness, &c. Thus Origen found a version of the Bible preserved at Jericho. (Calmet) — After the captivity, the owners shall claim their land."],["haydock",17,null,"haydock","Alas. Hebrew ahah, ah! only once. Septuagint \"O, thou Being, Lord God.\" (Haydock) — Hard. Hebrew also, \"hidden, wonderful,\" &c. (Calmet) — \"When the gods perform, nothing appears incredible.\" Pindar, Pyt. x. See v. 27. Lu. i. 37. (Haydock)"],["haydock",18,null,"haydock","After them, to the third and fourth generation. Ex. xxxiv. 7. His mercy is more exalted. (Calmet)"],["haydock",20,null,"haydock","Day. We remember thy ancient miracles, and witness others."],["haydock",23,null,"haydock","Any of. Literally \"all those,\" which seems better, as he who offends in one, becomes guilty of all; and the Jews certainly had observed some precepts, though they did not persevere unto the end. (Haydock)"],["haydock",24,null,"haydock","By. Literally \"at the presence of the sword,\" or by its force. Ps. lix. 6. (Worthington)"],["haydock",25,null,"haydock","Is given, in the eternal decrees. v. 28. (Haydock)"],["haydock",29,null,"haydock","Baal, the sun, &c. C. xix. 13. Soph. i. 5. and 4 K. xxiii. 12. (Calmet)"],["haydock",30,null,"haydock","Continually, (Sym.) \"moreover,\" (Aq.) or \"alone.\" Septuagint and Theodot. v. 23. (Haydock)"],["haydock",31,null,"haydock","Built it. The Jebusites polluted it. David himself offended, as well as Solomon, and under all the kings many crimes were committed, (Calmet) which is always the case in large cities. These general terms only denote a great corruption, tough some would be virtuous. (Haydock)"],["haydock",34,null,"haydock","It, under Achaz, Manasses, and perhaps the last kings of Juda. Ezec. viii. 9."],["haydock",35,null,"haydock","Consecrate. Hebrew \"make them pass\" through or into the fire, to be consumed in honour of Moloc. 4 K. xxi. 6. — Heart. They perhaps meant to imitate Abraham. C. vii. 31."],["haydock",37,null,"haydock","Lands. What is here said must be understood of the captives, yet in such a manner that Christians are more particularly designated, for the Jews never enjoyed such perfect security, nor followed one way, nor enjoyed the eternal covenant, (v. 39. 40. C. xxxi. 31.; Calmet) except those who embraced the true faith of Christ. At all times one religion alone can be pleasing to God, (v. 39.) and those who were saved under the old law, must have believed in a Redeemer to come, as we must do in one already past. (Haydock) — God would not suffer true believers to become extinct under the old law; much less will he since Christ's coming. (Worthington)"],["haydock",41,null,"haydock","Truth, like a tree which shall not be eradicated."],["haydock",44,null,"haydock","Juda, and in. A farther description of these cities is given, situated about the capital, Hebron, Sephala, ( the plain) near Gaza, (Calmet) and the Negeb or south country, (Haydock) towards Arabia. Here the excellent commentary of S. Jerom ends, to our great loss. (Calmet) — It was perhaps his last work, undertaken after the twelve minor and the other three great prophets. He never wrote on Baruch, on the epistle of Jeremias, the Gr. parts of Daniel, &c. as the Jews did not allow them to be canonical, and the point was not then decided. The Church styles S. Jerom \"the greatest doctor in expounding the sacred Scriptures,\" as he translated and illustrated the greatest part of them; (Haydock) and indeed he seems to have been the best qualified for the undertaking, of all the fathers. Du Pin. — Hence we may lament the more that we have not all his works in their genuine purity and perfection, as even the latest editions are very faulty, and proceed upon a plan which the learned do not approve. See A. Butler, Kennicott, &c. (Haydock)"]],"au":{"haydock":["George Leo Haydock",0,0,1849,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/07160a.htm"]},"fa":[[9,null,0,"The Christian Topography","Book 5",0,550,"This is Jeremiah the tenth in order, who was also privileged to prophesy concerning the mystery respecting Christ, saying thus: And they took the thirty pieces of silver, the price of him that was priced, whom certain of the sons of Israel did price, and they gave them for the potter's field as the Lord appointed me. The evangelist Matthew mentions this passage as having been fulfilled at the time of the passion."],[27,null,1,"Against Praxeas","AGAINST PRAXEAS 10",1,220,"Of course nothing is \"too hard for the Lord. But if we choose to apply this principle so extravagantly and harshly in our capricious imaginations, we may then make out God to have done anything we please, on the ground that it was not impossible for him to do it. We must not suppose, however, because he is able to do all things, that he has done what he has not done. But we must inquire whether he has really done it. God could, if he had liked, have furnished humankind with wings to fly with, just as he gave wings to kites. …"],[37,39,2,"Commentary on the Gospel of Luke",null,2,444,"… and which also He proclaimed of old by the voice of Ezechiel, thus speaking of those of the race of Israel; \"Behold, I will gather them from every land whither I have scattered them in My anger, and hot displeasure, and great wrath; and I will make them return to this place, and I will cause them to dwell safely, and they shall be to Me a people, and I will be to them a God, and I will give them another way and another heart, that they may fear Mo all their days. …"],[40,null,3,"A Treatise on the Predestination of the Saints","Book 2",3,430,"… For God has promised this, saying, \"I will put my fear in their hearts, that they may not depart from me. And what else is this than, \"Such and so great shall be my fear that I will put into their hearts that they will perseveringly cleave to me\"?...When, therefore, God's hand is upon Him, that we depart not from God, assuredly God's work reaches to us (for this is God's hand); by which work of God we are caused to be abiding in Christ with God—not, as in Adam, departing from God. For \"in Christ we have obtained a lot, being predestinated according to His purpose who worketh all things. …"],[40,null,3,"A Treatise Against Two Letters of the Pelagians","Book 4",3,430,"… They do not hear, \"Without me ye can do nothing;\" they do not hear, \"Love is of God;\" they do not hear, \"God hath dealt the measure of faith;\" they do not hear, \"The Spirit breatheth where it will,\" and, \"They who are led by the Spirit of God, they are the sons of God;\" they do not hear, \"No one can come unto me, unless it were given him of my Father;\" they do not hear what Esdras writes, \"Blessed is the Lord of our fathers, who hath put into the heart of the king to glorify His house which is in Jerusalem;\" they do not hear what the Lord says by Jeremiah, \"And I will put my fear …"],[40,null,3,"A Treatise on the Predestination of the Saints","Book 2",3,430,"… For Christ altogether with His members is—for the Church's sake, which is His body—the fulness of Him. When, therefore, God's hand is upon Him, that we depart not from God, assuredly God's work reaches to us (for this is God's hand); by which work of God we are caused to be abiding in Christ with God—not, as in Adam, departing from God. For \"in Christ we have obtained a lot, being predestinated according to His purpose who worketh all things. This, therefore, is God's hand, not ours, that we depart not from God. That, I say, is His hand who said, \"I will put my fear in their hearts, that they depart not from me.\""]],"fau":[["cosmas-indicopleustes","Cosmas Indicopleustes",0,"ecclesiastical-writer",null,0],["tertullian","Tertullian",0,"ecclesiastical-writer",null,0],["cyril-alexandria","Cyril of Alexandria",1,"father",null,1,444],["augustine","Augustine of Hippo",1,"father",null,1,430]],"ftr":[["J. W. McCrindle, Hakluyt Society (1897)"],["Ante-Nicene Fathers, vol. 3 (1885)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/anf03"],["R. Payne Smith (Oxford, 1859); transcribed and partly modernized by Roger Pearse"],["Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, series 1, vol. 5 (1887)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf105"]]}